  "groups": [
    {
      "id": "gleich",
      "regel": [
        "Many short words come in two forms: with a written accent and without one. **The accent changes the word**: *da* = from, *dà* = gives.",
        "The letter **h** is silent, so *a* and *ha*, *o* and *ho*, *anno* and *hanno* sound the same. Ask what kind of word you need: the verb *avere* is the one with the **h**.",
        "The accent goes on the last letter: *è, sì, là, tè*. Read the whole sentence to choose."
      ],
      "regelTr": {},
      "title": "Sounds the same, spelt differently",
      "lead": "You can't hear the difference, or hardly at all. In writing you see it at once.",
      "pairs": [
        {
          "id": "a-ha",
          "level": "A1",
          "a": {
            "w": "a",
            "pos": "preposition: to, at",
            "ex": "Vado <b>a</b> Roma."
          },
          "b": {
            "w": "ha",
            "pos": "verb avere: has",
            "ex": "Lui <b>ha</b> fame."
          },
          "why": "ha, with an h, is the verb avere (he/she has). a without h is the preposition: a Roma, a casa.",
          "tr": {},
          "check": {
            "gap": "Marco ___ due figli.",
            "answer": "b"
          },
          "variety": "it-IT"
        },
        {
          "id": "o-ho",
